        - A core need is to give high-quality feedback to the AI.  The highest quality
          feedback is program output, error messages, and debugger traces.  When Mike is
          updated to the latest version of the code, he can take such output and
          virtually always pinpoint where the problem is and write a solution for you,
          far faster than you can do yourself.
- Weaknesses
    - "Length of memory" in the context.  This is a weakness, particularly for the free
      AI services I'm currently using.  Workarounds:
        - I keep a file with my code formatting requirements; at least once per day I
          upload this file to Mike to refresh his memory
        - I stop and upload the current code we're working on to ensure we're talking
          about the same code.  It's possible the commercial tools have e.g. shared
          filesystems, but all I have currently is a browser window to type into
    - Can't trust their code blindly, similar to a human's coding
        - Not surprising, as their patterns come from human patterns
        - An absolute core need for you is to write impeccably good test cases to test
          their code at the corners and seams.  Good test cases takes good creativity,
          imagination, deep knowledge of the language and its weaknesses, and lots of
          experience.
        - The better the specifications (prompt), the better the results
        - With at least the free AI I use, a surprise was to find the AI is unable to
          run the specific python version I was using or the typical tools.  Early on,
          I'd get frustrated because I'd get back broken code.  The self-tests helped me
          find it quickly, but it was regression and it was annoying ("we already fixed
          this problem!").  You can't get angry; you just send back the error messages
          and debugger traces and the AI will figure it out.
        - In the Num project (Noether REPL), Mike probably wrote 99% of the code.  When
          things got complicated enough, his context would forget things he did
          previously and not use his old code or make comments like "you did this, but
          this is a weakness", when it was his code all along.  Ignore that, but an
          important thing to do is to ask "him" to do a code review occasionally and
          look for weaknesses.  This can be surprisingly powerful, as he'll almost
          always improve his own code and find corner case bugs that haven't been hit
          yet.
        - I found in the Noether REPL code (an abstract number class to do math with
          integers, rationals, floating point, complex numbers, and complex numbers with
          uncertainty) that there were numerous subtle logical bugs in the AI generated
          math stuff.  Though the architecture was good, the AI hasn't done calculations
          with such things for 50 years like I have and I'd step through the debugger to
