            arb.add_primitive("cat")

            num_dogs = Num("7 dog")
            num_cats = Num("12 cat")
            dog_food_rate = Num("0.2 kg/dog")
            cat_food_rate = Num("0.1 kg/cat")

            if not error:
                # Correct physics: (dog * kg/dog) + (cat * kg/cat) = kg
                food_mass = num_dogs * dog_food_rate + num_cats * cat_food_rate
            else:
                # Logical error: Trying to add (cat * kg/dog) to (dog * kg/cat)
                food_mass = num_cats * dog_food_rate + num_dogs * cat_food_rate

            print(f"Amount of food = {food_mass}")

    When you run the error=True version, the interpreter doesn't just give you a wrong
    answer, it refuses to proceed. It throws a "ValueError: Incompatible dimensions:
    (dog)*(kg/cat) vs (cat)*(kg/dog)".

    The Thesis: Promoting the String to a Type

    We are "promoting" unit strings to semantic types. If a compiler can catch a syntax
    error, it should be able to catch a dimensional error.

    To an engineer, a unit is a vector. To a physicist, "5 m" is an invariant under a
    scaling transformation.  By stripping the units, we discard the very metadata that
    prevents half-billion-dollar mistakes (in 2026 dollars) like the Mars Climate Orbiter.
    This isn't just for physics; an ERP system could use this to prevent a million-dollar
    reconciliation error by ensuring "Unit_Price" and "Quantity" don't get swapped.

    The Vision: A Persistent Lab Notebook

    The tool we’re building isn't just a calculator; it’s a narrative history.

        Persistence: Every operation is logged to an SQLite "paper trail."

        Documentation as Metadata: Every number has a .d attribute. You can attach the "why"
        to the "what" directly in the REPL (read-evaluate-print loop).

        Semantic Flexibility: The REPL "learns" your units. If you're solving a problem in
        finance, "BasisPoint" becomes a primitive unit just as easily as "kilogram."

